<Vertical id="tmf_intro">
<Markdown id="tmf_intro_md">
# READ 1000

## Introduction

In this module, you will participate in a simulated small group discussion — the classmates and professors in it are not real people — in
READ 1000. Your goal is to use the skills you are learning in the course
(identifying key vocabulary; using academic language; synthesizing key ideas;
and thinking like a historian) to complete **Pulling It Together: four or five sentences comparing the
historic temperance movement with today's approaches to drinking less.** You'll
build it from the texts you read — the kind of comparison a U.S. History student makes.

You'll work with several texts for this project:

- a short **background lecture** on the temperance movement
- an **excerpt** from the *American Yawp* textbook (your main reading)
- a **magazine article** — your instructor hands this one out **on paper**, and
  you'll read and mark it there
- a **lithograph** (a kind of image) about the topic from the 19th century

You'll work with three simulated peers: **Danielle, Joe, and Karis.** Your
simulated Reading instructor is **Professor Booker.** Your simulated History
instructor is **Professor Hale.**

It will take about one hour to complete this module. It's required as part of
READ 1000, and you have until the date your instructor sets to complete it.

This module is a little bit different from participating in our READ 1000 class.
Danielle, Joe, Karis, Professor Booker, and Professor Hale are **not real
people,** but you can learn from and interact with them.

**About answering questions.** Most questions give you **one** submission: choose
your answer, press **Check,** and the conversation moves on whether you got it
right or not. Take your time before you press it — you won't be asked again.

All right, now imagine that you are in your classroom, but class has not started
yet. You are talking to Danielle, Joe, and Karis. Click **Continue** in the
conversation on the left to begin.
</Markdown>
</Vertical> <!-- tmf_intro -->


