<!-- p.25 — three highlight questions on the key sentence. The verb is a single
     word; the subject and the object are graded as WHOLE PHRASES — every word
     of the phrase and nothing outside it.

     Grading the phrase as a phrase is the lesson. The next two screens ask
     students to hold a noun phrase together as one unit; accepting the head
     word by itself here would teach the opposite on the screen before.

     Order is VERB → SUBJECT → OBJECT, not S-V-O. The verb is the one part
     you can find without having identified anything else; once you have it,
     "who did it" and "what got done to" are questions about the verb. Asking
     for the subject first asks students to find it out of nowhere. -->
<Vertical id="tmf_grammar_svo">
<Markdown id="tmf_grammar_svo_md">
## Verb, Subject, Object

Three quick highlights on the key sentence — the verb first, then the subject,
then the object.

The verb is a **single word**. The subject and the object are **whole phrases**:
highlight every word of the phrase, and nothing outside it.
</Markdown>

<CapaProblem id="tmf_grammar_svo_verb_problem" title="Highlight the verb" maxAttempts="1">
<TextSelectionGrader id="tmf_grammar_svo_verb_grader">
<TextSelectionInput id="tmf_grammar_svo_verb">
Highlight the verb — a single word.
---
Such intrusions by the Protestant middle class [exacerbated] class, ethnic, and religious tensions.
---
all: Right — "exacerbated" is the verb: it's what the intrusions did.
: The verb is the action word — what happened in this sentence?
</TextSelectionInput>
</TextSelectionGrader>
</CapaProblem> <!-- tmf_grammar_svo_verb_problem -->

<CapaProblem id="tmf_grammar_svo_subject_problem" title="Highlight the subject" maxAttempts="1">
<TextSelectionGrader id="tmf_grammar_svo_subject_grader">
<TextSelectionInput id="tmf_grammar_svo_subject">
Highlight the subject — the entire subject phrase, every word of it.
---
[Such intrusions by the Protestant middle class] exacerbated class, ethnic, and religious tensions.
---
all: Right — the subject is the whole phrase "Such intrusions by the Protestant middle class." Its head noun is "intrusions"; the rest of the phrase tells you which intrusions.
: The subject is who or what does the action. Find the noun that did the exacerbating, then take the whole phrase built around it.
</TextSelectionInput>
</TextSelectionGrader>
</CapaProblem> <!-- tmf_grammar_svo_subject_problem -->

<!-- The trailing period is {optional}: a student sweeping to the end of the
     sentence catches it, and stopping a highlight one character short of a
     period is not a grammar mistake. -->
<CapaProblem id="tmf_grammar_svo_object_problem" title="Highlight the object" maxAttempts="1">
<TextSelectionGrader id="tmf_grammar_svo_object_grader">
<TextSelectionInput id="tmf_grammar_svo_object">
Highlight the direct object — the entire object phrase, every word of it.
---
Such intrusions by the Protestant middle class exacerbated [class, ethnic, and religious tensions]{.}
---
all: Right — the object is the whole phrase "class, ethnic, and religious tensions." Its head noun is "tensions"; the words in front of it say which tensions.
: What did the intrusions exacerbate? Find that noun, then take the whole phrase built around it.
</TextSelectionInput>
</TextSelectionGrader>
</CapaProblem> <!-- tmf_grammar_svo_object_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- tmf_grammar_svo -->

<!-- p.26 — inside the subject NP. Three sequential highlights, matching the
     SVO screen's one-question-at-a-time pattern: asking for premodifier, head
     noun, and postmodifier all at once in a single selection gave a student no
     way to be partly right and no feedback until all three landed.

     The text shown is ONLY the noun phrase, not the whole sentence: the rest
     of the sentence is nothing but a place to make mistakes here.

     "Determiner," not "premodifier" — it is the word the course actually uses
     for "Such," and it names a category rather than a position. -->
<Vertical id="tmf_grammar_np">
<Markdown id="tmf_grammar_np_md">
## Inside the Noun Phrase

The subject of that sentence is a noun phrase all by itself. Here it is on its
own — take it apart in three steps.
</Markdown>

<SimpleTextSelection id="tmf_grammar_np_head" mode="graded" title="Highlight the head noun">
Highlight the head noun — the one word the whole phrase is built around.
---
Such [intrusions] by the Protestant middle class
---
all: Right — "intrusions" is the head noun. Everything else in the phrase is there to tell you which intrusions.
: It is a single word, and it is the thing the phrase is naming. Which one word could stand in for the whole phrase?
</SimpleTextSelection>

<SimpleTextSelection id="tmf_grammar_np_det" mode="graded" title="Highlight the determiner">
Highlight the determiner — the word in front of the head noun that points at which ones.
---
[Such] intrusions by the Protestant middle class
---
all: Right — "Such" is the determiner. It points back at intrusions already described, which is why the next task is finding them.
: It comes before the head noun, and words like "the," "these," "his," and "such" do this job.
</SimpleTextSelection>

<SimpleTextSelection id="tmf_grammar_np_post" mode="graded" title="Highlight the postmodifier">
Highlight the postmodifier — the part after the head noun that tells you more about it.
---
Such intrusions [by the Protestant middle class]
---
all: Right — "by the Protestant middle class" is the postmodifier. It tells you whose intrusions they were.
: It follows the head noun, and here it is a whole phrase rather than a single word.
</SimpleTextSelection>
</Vertical> <!-- tmf_grammar_np -->

<!-- p.27 — the structure, laid out (info screen) -->
<Vertical id="tmf_grammar_structure">
<Markdown id="tmf_grammar_structure_md">
## The Grammatical Structure

Here is the sentence laid out:

> **[ Such intrusions by the Protestant middle class ]** — subject (a noun phrase)
> **exacerbated** — verb
> **[ class, ethnic, and religious tensions ]** — direct object (a noun phrase)

Both the subject and the object are **noun phrases** — to understand the
sentence, you hold each whole phrase together.
</Markdown>
<Done id="tmf_grammar_structure_done" label="I understand" />
</Vertical>

<!-- p.28 — highlight the antecedent of "Such intrusions" -->
<SimpleTextSelection id="tmf_grammar_antecedent" mode="graded" title="Find the Antecedent">
"Such intrusions" points back to an earlier sentence. Find the sentence that describes those intrusions — its antecedent — and highlight the entire sentence, from its first word through its final period.
---
In response to the perception that heavy drinking was associated with men who abused, abandoned, or neglected their family obligations, women formed a significant presence in societies dedicated to eradicating liquor. Temperance became a hallmark of middle-class respectability among both men and women and developed into a crusade with a visible class character. [Temperance, like many other reform efforts, was championed by the middle class and threatened to intrude on the private lives of lower-class workers, many of whom were Irish Catholics.] Such intrusions by the Protestant middle class exacerbated class, ethnic, and religious tensions. Still, while the temperance movement made less substantial inroads into lower-class workers' drinking culture, the movement was still a great success for reformers.
---
all: Yes — that earlier sentence describes the intrusion (the middle class threatening to intrude on lower-class, largely Irish Catholic workers) that "Such intrusions" refers back to.
: "Such intrusions" must point to an intrusion already named. Find the sentence that describes the middle class intruding on workers' lives.
</SimpleTextSelection>


