<!-- CHAT-EMBEDDED — the five comprehension questions -->

<MarkupProblem id="tmf_comp_q1" title="Question 1"><![CDATA[
>>How did the Second Great Awakening address the rapid social and economic changes of the post-independence era?<<

( ) By advocating for the total prohibition of all commercial distilleries.
( ) By promoting traditional Protestant Christianity while rejecting reform movements.
(x) By revitalizing Christian spirits and inspiring personal and social reform.
( ) By discouraging middle-class involvement in charitable organizations.

[explanation]
The passage calls the Second Great Awakening "a spiritual response to such changes, revitalizing Christian spirits while also providing an institutional antidote... by inspiring both personal and social reform."
[/explanation]
]]></MarkupProblem>

<MarkupProblem id="tmf_comp_q2" title="Question 2"><![CDATA[
>>Why were middle-class evangelicals able to devote significant time and resources to reform campaigns?<<

(x) The Industrial Revolution provided them with the necessary time and resources.
( ) They were financially supported by churches and the government.
( ) They were mandated to do so by the American Tract Society.
( ) Working-class laborers funded their reform efforts.

[explanation]
"Because of the Industrial Revolution, middle-class evangelicals had time and resources to devote to reform campaigns."
[/explanation]
]]></MarkupProblem>

<MarkupProblem id="tmf_comp_q3" title="Question 3"><![CDATA[
>>How did temperance reformers shift their approach as alcoholism became an increasingly visible issue in towns and cities?<<

( ) They shifted from demanding total prohibition to encouraging moderate consumption.
( ) They focused exclusively on taxing commercial distilleries rather than trying to get people to stop drinking.
(x) They shifted from advocating moderate drinking to demanding full abstinence from all alcohol.
( ) They stopped seeking legal bans on liquor sales altogether.

[explanation]
"Most reformers escalated their efforts from advocating moderation in liquor consumption to full abstinence from all alcohol."
[/explanation]
]]></MarkupProblem>

<MarkupProblem id="tmf_comp_q4" title="Question 4"><![CDATA[
>>What primary concern prompted many women to join societies dedicated to stopping drinking alcohol?<<

( ) They could no longer afford to pay the high taxes imposed on household dairy products like milk.
(x) They believed that heavy drinking was linked to men abusing, abandoning, or neglecting their families.
( ) The women wanted to work in commercial distilleries, but it was against the law.
( ) The women were opposed to the preaching of Lyman Beecher and the work of the American Tract Society.

[explanation]
Women formed a presence in these societies "in response to the perception that heavy drinking was associated with men who abused, abandoned, or neglected their family obligations."
[/explanation]
]]></MarkupProblem>

<MarkupProblem id="tmf_comp_q5" title="Question 5"><![CDATA[
>>What evidence from the text demonstrates the ultimate success of the temperance movement by the 1840s?<<

( ) Alcohol consumption completely ceased across all social classes.
( ) All state legislatures permanently banned the production of alcohol.
( ) Lower-class workers adopted Protestant drinking culture.
(x) Americans in the 1840s drank half of what they had in the 1820s.

[explanation]
"In the 1840s, Americans drank half of what they had in the 1820s, and per capita consumption continued to decline over the next two decades."
[/explanation]
]]></MarkupProblem>


