     CHAT-EMBEDDED — vocabulary, chunk A / para 1 (PDF p.15-17)
     ============================================================ -->

<!-- Each chat-embedded question is wrapped in a Vertical that TAKES OVER the
     embed id (`::tmf_q_commerce_screen`), so the correctness feedback under
     the choices renders inside the chat bubble with them. The ChoiceInput
     keeps its own id — every wait, and every readback, still names it.

     Inside that Vertical the question is a one-attempt CapaProblem
     (`<id>_problem`, maxAttempts="1") around a KeyGrader around the
     ChoiceInput: this is an assessment, and each item is answered once. The
     Check button is the CapaProblem's own footer. The ChoiceInput id and every
     option value are unchanged — the chatpeg gates and the analytics read
     them. maxAttempts is spelled out because "0" means unlimited, not none.

     Every per-choice verdict below is gated on `<id>_problem.submitCount` as
     well as on the chosen value. Keyed on the value alone, a student with a
     Check button in front of them could click each option in turn and read
     which one comes back "Yes" before ever submitting — the whole item,
     answered for them, under a rule that allows one attempt. The feedback
     text is unchanged; it is now the explanation that follows the answer. -->

<Vertical id="tmf_q_commerce_screen">
<CapaProblem id="tmf_q_commerce_problem" title="Vocabulary: commerce" maxAttempts="1">
<KeyGrader id="tmf_q_commerce_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="tmf_q_commerce">
<Markdown>
Look at the highlighted sentence and choose the best definition for **commerce**:

> "…the Industrial Revolution had led to a host of social problems associated
> with urban growth and large-scale **commerce**."
</Markdown>
  <Distractor value="church_formation"> The way Christianity formed in the U.S.        </Distractor>
  <Distractor value="population_study"> The study of how urban populations grow.       </Distractor>
         <Key value="buying_selling"  > The activities of buying and selling products. </Key>
  <Distractor value="social_laws"     > The laws people make to limit social problems. </Distractor>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<Markdown when="@tmf_q_commerce.value === 'church_formation' &amp;&amp; @tmf_q_commerce_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Not quite — that's the religious half of the paragraph. "Large-scale commerce" sits next to "urban growth" — both are economic changes the Industrial Revolution brought.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmf_q_commerce.value === 'population_study' &amp;&amp; @tmf_q_commerce_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Not quite — that's the "urban growth" half of the sentence. "Commerce" is the other half — trade, not counting people.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmf_q_commerce.value === 'social_laws' &amp;&amp; @tmf_q_commerce_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Not quite — laws come later in the chapter, with prohibition. "Commerce" names the economic activity itself, not the rules made about it.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmf_q_commerce.value === 'buying_selling' &amp;&amp; @tmf_q_commerce_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Yes — commerce is buying and selling. "Large-scale commerce" means trade grown big enough to reshape whole cities.*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- tmf_q_commerce_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- tmf_q_commerce_screen -->

<Vertical id="tmf_q_antidote_screen">
<CapaProblem id="tmf_q_antidote_problem" title="Vocabulary: antidote" maxAttempts="1">
<KeyGrader id="tmf_q_antidote_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="tmf_q_antidote">
<Markdown>
Choose the best definition for **antidote**:

> "The Second Great Awakening was in part a spiritual response to such changes…
> providing an institutional **antidote** to the insecurities of a rapidly
> changing world."
</Markdown>
  <Distractor value="anecdote_story"     > A short story about a real person or event.     </Distractor>
  <Distractor value="poison_medicine"    > A medicine that saves you from poison.          </Distractor>
  <Distractor value="unpublished_writing"> An unpublished writing.                         </Distractor>
         <Key value="counteracts_harm"   > Something that counteracts a harmful situation. </Key>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<!-- The peers unpack the two "anecdote" definitions and the medicine reading in
   the dialogue right after this, so the feedback here stays short. -->
<Markdown when="@tmf_q_antidote.value === 'anecdote_story' &amp;&amp; @tmf_q_antidote_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Not quite — that's an **anecdote** — one letter off, an entirely different word.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmf_q_antidote.value === 'unpublished_writing' &amp;&amp; @tmf_q_antidote_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Not quite — also a definition of some other word — nothing in "antidote" is about writing.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmf_q_antidote.value === 'poison_medicine' &amp;&amp; @tmf_q_antidote_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Not quite — a real meaning of the word, but not this one: the Awakening was no literal medicine. Read it figuratively.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmf_q_antidote.value === 'counteracts_harm' &amp;&amp; @tmf_q_antidote_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Right — the figurative sense: something that works against a harm. Here, against the insecurities of a changing world.*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- tmf_q_antidote_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- tmf_q_antidote_screen -->

<Vertical id="tmf_q_proliferated_screen">
<CapaProblem id="tmf_q_proliferated_problem" title="Vocabulary: proliferated" maxAttempts="1">
<KeyGrader id="tmf_q_proliferated_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="tmf_q_proliferated">
<Markdown>
Choose the best definition for **proliferated**:

> "These new societies **proliferated** in the United States between 1815 and
> 1861…"
</Markdown>
         <Key value="increased_rapidly"  > Increased rapidly in numbers.                </Key>
  <Distractor value="merged_into_one"    > Came together to form a single organization. </Distractor>
  <Distractor value="decreased_influence"> Decreased rapidly in influence.              </Distractor>
  <Distractor value="moved_to_cities"    > Moved from farms to major cities.            </Distractor>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<Markdown when="@tmf_q_proliferated.value === 'merged_into_one' &amp;&amp; @tmf_q_proliferated_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Not quite — nearly the opposite: these societies multiplied and spread rather than folding into one body.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmf_q_proliferated.value === 'decreased_influence' &amp;&amp; @tmf_q_proliferated_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Not quite — "Rapidly" is right; the direction isn't. Between 1815 and 1861 the societies were growing, not fading.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmf_q_proliferated.value === 'moved_to_cities' &amp;&amp; @tmf_q_proliferated_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Not quite — that's the urban growth from the paragraph before. "Proliferated" is about number, not location.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmf_q_proliferated.value === 'increased_rapidly' &amp;&amp; @tmf_q_proliferated_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Yes — to proliferate is to multiply fast. The societies didn't just appear; they kept appearing.*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- tmf_q_proliferated_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- tmf_q_proliferated_screen -->

<!-- CHAT-EMBEDDED — vocabulary, chunk B / para 3 (PDF p.18) -->

<Vertical id="tmf_q_galvanized_screen">
<CapaProblem id="tmf_q_galvanized_problem" title="Vocabulary: galvanized" maxAttempts="1">
<KeyGrader id="tmf_q_galvanized_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="tmf_q_galvanized">
<Markdown>
What is the best definition of **galvanized**?

> "…the movement's effort to curb the consumption of alcohol **galvanized**
> widespread support among the middle class."
</Markdown>
  <Distractor value="gradually_built"    > To build up slowly, a little at a time. </Distractor>
         <Key value="spurred_into_action"> To spur into action; to energize.       </Key>
  <Distractor value="zinc_coating"       > To coat iron or steel with zinc.        </Distractor>
  <Distractor value="wore_down"          > To discourage or wear down.             </Distractor>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<!-- Karis and Danielle name the figurative definition in the dialogue that
   follows, so these lines only have to get a student there. The old options
   were all worded around "electric shock," which sent students hunting for a
   literal current instead of reading the sentence; the shock framing is gone. -->
<Markdown when="@tmf_q_galvanized.value === 'gradually_built' &amp;&amp; @tmf_q_galvanized_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Not quite — watch the pace. Galvanized support arrives all at once; something that builds up slowly is the opposite.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmf_q_galvanized.value === 'zinc_coating' &amp;&amp; @tmf_q_galvanized_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Not quite — that's galvanized steel, a real but different sense of the word. The support wasn't coated in metal.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmf_q_galvanized.value === 'wore_down' &amp;&amp; @tmf_q_galvanized_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Not quite — backwards. The sentence says the movement's effort won widespread support, not that it wore support down.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmf_q_galvanized.value === 'spurred_into_action' &amp;&amp; @tmf_q_galvanized_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Right — the effort spurred the middle class into action and energized their support.*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- tmf_q_galvanized_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- tmf_q_galvanized_screen -->

<!-- CHAT-EMBEDDED — vocabulary, chunk C / para 4 (PDF p.19-21) -->

<Vertical id="tmf_q_eradicating_screen">
<CapaProblem id="tmf_q_eradicating_problem" title="Vocabulary: eradicating" maxAttempts="1">
<KeyGrader id="tmf_q_eradicating_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="tmf_q_eradicating">
<Markdown>
What words could take the place of **eradicating** — but keep the meaning the same?

> "…women formed a significant presence in societies dedicated to
> **eradicating** liquor."
</Markdown>
  <Distractor value="making_rules"       > making rules for     </Distractor>
  <Distractor value="advertising"        > helping to advertise </Distractor>
         <Key value="completely_removing"> completely removing  </Key>
  <Distractor value="keeping_safe"       > keeping safe from    </Distractor>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<Markdown when="@tmf_q_eradicating.value === 'making_rules' &amp;&amp; @tmf_q_eradicating_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Not quite — regulating liquor is a milder goal than the one the sentence describes. These societies wanted it gone, not managed.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmf_q_eradicating.value === 'advertising' &amp;&amp; @tmf_q_eradicating_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Not quite — the opposite of what a temperance society was dedicated to.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmf_q_eradicating.value === 'keeping_safe' &amp;&amp; @tmf_q_eradicating_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Not quite — read it back in: "dedicated to keeping liquor safe" turns the sentence around. The societies were against it.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmf_q_eradicating.value === 'completely_removing' &amp;&amp; @tmf_q_eradicating_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Yes — to eradicate is to root out entirely. That's why it goes with "full abstinence" rather than "moderation."*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- tmf_q_eradicating_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- tmf_q_eradicating_screen -->

<Vertical id="tmf_q_exacerbated_screen">
<CapaProblem id="tmf_q_exacerbated_problem" title="Vocabulary: exacerbated" maxAttempts="1">
<KeyGrader id="tmf_q_exacerbated_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="tmf_q_exacerbated">
<Markdown>
What words could take the place of **exacerbated** — but keep the meaning the same?

> "Such intrusions by the Protestant middle class **exacerbated** class, ethnic,
> and religious tensions."
</Markdown>
  <Distractor value="calmed_down" > calmed down completely </Distractor>
         <Key value="made_worse"  > made much worse        </Key>
  <Distractor value="fixed"       > fixed right up         </Distractor>
  <Distractor value="started_over"> started over           </Distractor>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<Markdown when="@tmf_q_exacerbated.value === 'calmed_down' &amp;&amp; @tmf_q_exacerbated_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Not quite — backwards. Read it into the sentence: if the intrusions had calmed things, the paragraph wouldn't be about tensions at all.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmf_q_exacerbated.value === 'fixed' &amp;&amp; @tmf_q_exacerbated_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Not quite — also the wrong direction — nothing here gets repaired. The paragraph is describing friction, not its cure.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmf_q_exacerbated.value === 'started_over' &amp;&amp; @tmf_q_exacerbated_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Not quite — the class, ethnic, and religious tensions were already there. The intrusions changed their degree, not their starting point.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmf_q_exacerbated.value === 'made_worse' &amp;&amp; @tmf_q_exacerbated_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Right — to exacerbate is to make something bad that already exists worse.*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- tmf_q_exacerbated_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- tmf_q_exacerbated_screen -->

<Vertical id="tmf_q_per_capita_screen">
<CapaProblem id="tmf_q_per_capita_problem" title="Vocabulary: per capita" maxAttempts="1">
<KeyGrader id="tmf_q_per_capita_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="tmf_q_per_capita">
<Markdown>
What words could take the place of **per capita consumption** — but keep the meaning the same?

> "In the 1840s, Americans drank half of what they had in the 1820s, and
> **per capita** consumption continued to decline over the next two decades."
</Markdown>
  <Distractor value="per_saloon"      > the amount of drinking within each saloon       </Distractor>
  <Distractor value="nationwide_total"> the amount of drinking across the whole country </Distractor>
  <Distractor value="per_ethnic_group"> the amount of drinking by each ethnic group     </Distractor>
         <Key value="per_person"      > the average amount of drinking per person       </Key>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<Markdown when="@tmf_q_per_capita.value === 'per_saloon' &amp;&amp; @tmf_q_per_capita_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Not quite — "Capita" is Latin for heads — people, not places.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmf_q_per_capita.value === 'nationwide_total' &amp;&amp; @tmf_q_per_capita_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Not quite — a national total would climb just because the population climbed. Per capita divides by the people, which is what makes the drop meaningful.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmf_q_per_capita.value === 'per_ethnic_group' &amp;&amp; @tmf_q_per_capita_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Not quite — groups do show up elsewhere in this paragraph, but "per capita" counts by head — one person at a time.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmf_q_per_capita.value === 'per_person' &amp;&amp; @tmf_q_per_capita_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Yes — the average per person. Take all the alcohol drunk in the country and divide it by everyone in it: the average American in the 1840s drank half of what the average American drank in the 1820s.*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- tmf_q_per_capita_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- tmf_q_per_capita_screen -->


<!-- ============================================================
     CHAT-EMBEDDED — Benevolent Empire, disciplinary term (PDF p.22-24)
     ============================================================ -->

<Vertical id="tmf_be_term_screen">
<CapaProblem id="tmf_be_term_problem" title="Benevolent Empire: the term" maxAttempts="1">
<KeyGrader id="tmf_be_term_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="tmf_be_term">
<Markdown>
**Benevolent Empire** is a discipline-specific term. Using clues in the passage,
which word or phrase most directly helps us understand what it was?
</Markdown>
  <Distractor value="traditional_religion" > traditional religion  </Distractor>
         <Key value="powerful_network"     > powerful network      </Key>
  <Distractor value="industrial_revolution"> Industrial Revolution </Distractor>
  <Distractor value="spiritual_response"   > spiritual response    </Distractor>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<Markdown when="@tmf_be_term.value === 'traditional_religion' &amp;&amp; @tmf_be_term_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Not quite — the passage says traditional Protestantism was "at low tide." The Benevolent Empire is the new thing, not the old one.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmf_be_term.value === 'industrial_revolution' &amp;&amp; @tmf_be_term_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Not quite — that's the cause of the social problems the reformers answered — not a name for the reformers' network.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmf_be_term.value === 'spiritual_response' &amp;&amp; @tmf_be_term_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Not quite — that phrase describes the Second Great Awakening. The Benevolent Empire is the organized thing the Awakening produced.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmf_be_term.value === 'powerful_network' &amp;&amp; @tmf_be_term_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Yes — the passage hands you the phrase: "a powerful network." Many societies, linked together, melding religion and reform.*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- tmf_be_term_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- tmf_be_term_screen -->

<Vertical id="tmf_be_connect_screen">
<CapaProblem id="tmf_be_connect_problem" title="Benevolent Empire: what connects to it" maxAttempts="1">
<CheckboxGrader id="tmf_be_connect_grader">
<CheckboxInput id="tmf_be_connect">
<Markdown>
The Benevolent Empire was, indeed, a **powerful network.** What other words or
phrases from the chapter describe or connect to it? **Select all that apply.**
</Markdown>
         <Key value="societies_movements"    > societies / movements   </Key>
         <Key value="middle_class"           > middle-class            </Key>
  <Distractor value="american_independence"  > American independence   </Distractor>
  <Distractor value="commercial_distilleries"> commercial distilleries </Distractor>
         <Key value="religion_and_reform"    > religion and reform     </Key>
  <Distractor value="irish_catholics"        > Irish Catholics         </Distractor>
</CheckboxInput>
</CheckboxGrader>

<!-- Multi-select, so the feedback has to cover three cases: a wrong pick, a
   right-but-incomplete set, and the whole set.

   SCORING RULE: exact match. CheckboxGrader with no partialCredit marks the
   response correct only when all three Keys and no Distractor are selected;
   anything else is simply wrong. That is a strict rule for a six-option
   select-all, and it is declared here as the rule this form scores by, not
   as the only defensible one — partial credit is the obvious next question. -->
<Markdown when="@tmf_be_connect.value.includes('american_independence') &amp;&amp; @tmf_be_connect_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Not that one — American independence is the era the chapter opens in. The backdrop, not a piece of the network.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmf_be_connect.value.includes('commercial_distilleries') &amp;&amp; @tmf_be_connect_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Not that one — distilleries are what the reformers were up against. Look for the words that describe the reformers themselves.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmf_be_connect.value.includes('irish_catholics') &amp;&amp; @tmf_be_connect_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Not that one — Irish Catholic workers are the people the middle-class reformers intruded on: the other side of the tension, not part of the network.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmf_be_connect.value.length &gt; 0 &amp;&amp; @tmf_be_connect.value.length &lt; 3 &amp;&amp; @tmf_be_connect_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Keep looking — more than one phrase here belongs to the network. Ask of each: who was in it, how were they organized, and what did it join together?*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmf_be_connect.value.length === 3 &amp;&amp; @tmf_be_connect.value.includes('societies_movements') &amp;&amp; @tmf_be_connect.value.includes('middle_class') &amp;&amp; @tmf_be_connect.value.includes('religion_and_reform') &amp;&amp; @tmf_be_connect_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*That's the set: middle-class people, organized into societies and movements, melding religion and reform.*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- tmf_be_connect_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- tmf_be_connect_screen -->

<Vertical id="tmf_be_bestdef_screen">
<CapaProblem id="tmf_be_bestdef_problem" title="Benevolent Empire: the best definition" maxAttempts="1">
<KeyGrader id="tmf_be_bestdef_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="tmf_be_bestdef">
<Markdown>
Each member of your group wrote a one-sentence description of the Benevolent
Empire. Read them and choose the **best** one.
</Markdown>
  <Distractor value="danielle_evangelicals_name"    > **Danielle:** The name given to the Evangelicals in the temperance movement after the Second Great Awakening.               </Distractor>
         <Key value="joe_network_religion_reform"   > **Joe:** A strong network of religious societies that brought together religion and reform among middle-class Evangelicals. </Key>
  <Distractor value="karis_reforms_promote_religion"> **Karis:** The set of reforms designed to promote religious ideas that had decreased after the Industrial Revolution.       </Distractor>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<Markdown when="@tmf_be_bestdef.value === 'danielle_evangelicals_name' &amp;&amp; @tmf_be_bestdef_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Not quite — Danielle names who was in it, but not what it **was** — and the Benevolent Empire was much wider than temperance alone.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmf_be_bestdef.value === 'karis_reforms_promote_religion' &amp;&amp; @tmf_be_bestdef_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Not quite — Karis has the engine running backwards. Religious revival came first and powered the reform, rather than reform being invented to prop religion back up.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmf_be_bestdef.value === 'joe_network_religion_reform' &amp;&amp; @tmf_be_bestdef_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Yes — Joe's has all three pieces: a network, made of societies, joining religion and reform among the middle class.*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- tmf_be_bestdef_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- tmf_be_bestdef_screen -->

<!-- CHAT-EMBEDDED — why did the intrusions exacerbate tensions? (PDF p.29) -->

<Vertical id="tmf_why_exacerbated_screen">
<CapaProblem id="tmf_why_exacerbated_problem" title="Why the intrusions exacerbated tensions" maxAttempts="1">
<KeyGrader id="tmf_why_exacerbated_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="tmf_why_exacerbated">
<Markdown>
So why did those intrusions — like the temperance campaigns — *exacerbate*
class, ethnic, and religious tensions?
</Markdown>
  <Distractor value="money_to_saloons"      > Because reformers gave money directly to saloon owners instead of working-class people.                       </Distractor>
  <Distractor value="only_irish_affected"   > Because the campaigns ended up affecting only the Irish Catholics but not the working-class people.           </Distractor>
  <Distractor value="irish_led_movement"    > Because Irish Catholic immigrants led the temperance movement themselves.                                     </Distractor>
         <Key value="middle_class_intrusion"> Because middle-class Protestant reformers intruded on the customs of immigrant and working-class communities. </Key>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<Markdown when="@tmf_why_exacerbated.value === 'money_to_saloons' &amp;&amp; @tmf_why_exacerbated_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Not quite — no money changes hands in this passage, and money isn't what the tension is about.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmf_why_exacerbated.value === 'only_irish_affected' &amp;&amp; @tmf_why_exacerbated_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Not quite — the sentence names Irish Catholics as many of the lower-class workers, not as the only people affected. The friction runs between classes.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmf_why_exacerbated.value === 'irish_led_movement' &amp;&amp; @tmf_why_exacerbated_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Not quite — turn it around: temperance was "championed by the middle class," and it threatened to intrude on lower-class workers, many of whom were Irish Catholics.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmf_why_exacerbated.value === 'middle_class_intrusion' &amp;&amp; @tmf_why_exacerbated_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Yes — one group reaching into another group's private life is exactly what makes class, ethnic, and religious tensions worse.*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- tmf_why_exacerbated_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- tmf_why_exacerbated_screen -->


<!-- ============================================================
     CHAT-EMBEDDED — article vocabulary (PDF p.37-39)
     ============================================================ -->

<Vertical id="tmf_q_teetotaling_screen">
<CapaProblem id="tmf_q_teetotaling_problem" title="Vocabulary: teetotaling" maxAttempts="1">
<KeyGrader id="tmf_q_teetotaling_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="tmf_q_teetotaling">
<Markdown>
Dr. Booker asked us to think about **self-optimization**. It's in this sentence:

> "She considers modern **teetotaling** to be steered by a great moral project
> of our age: self-optimization."

First — what does **teetotaling** mean?
</Markdown>
  <Distractor value="heavy_drinking"   > drinking too much alcohol                        </Distractor>
  <Distractor value="persuading_others"> convincing other people to stop drinking alcohol </Distractor>
  <Distractor value="drinking_tea"     > replacing alcohol with tea or coffee             </Distractor>
         <Key value="no_alcohol_at_all"> not drinking alcohol at all                      </Key>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<Markdown when="@tmf_q_teetotaling.value === 'heavy_drinking' &amp;&amp; @tmf_q_teetotaling_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Not quite — the opposite. Teetotaling is on the abstaining side of the question.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmf_q_teetotaling.value === 'persuading_others' &amp;&amp; @tmf_q_teetotaling_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Not quite — that's what a movement does. Teetotaling is what one person does — about their own drinking.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmf_q_teetotaling.value === 'drinking_tea' &amp;&amp; @tmf_q_teetotaling_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Not quite — a reasonable guess from the spelling, but the "tee" is an emphatic T, for **total** — not tea.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmf_q_teetotaling.value === 'no_alcohol_at_all' &amp;&amp; @tmf_q_teetotaling_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Right — total abstinence, none at all. That's the "total" hiding inside teetotal.*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- tmf_q_teetotaling_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- tmf_q_teetotaling_screen -->

<Vertical id="tmf_selfoptim">
<CapaProblem id="tmf_selfoptim_pick_problem" title="Self-optimization: the best sentence" maxAttempts="1">
<KeyGrader id="tmf_selfoptim_pick_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="tmf_selfoptim_pick">
<Markdown>
To pin down what **self-optimization** means, each of us found a sentence from
the article that gives it context. Who picked the **best** one?
</Markdown>
  <Distractor value="danielle_health_research" > **Danielle:** "(To be fair, this focus on health is partially a response to research on moderate alcohol consumption's detrimental effects on heart health, cancer risk, and lifespan.)" </Distractor>
  <Distractor value="karis_freedom_to_choose"  > **Karis:** "Sober-curiosity, she said, was about a person's 'freedom to choose.'"                                                                                                        </Distractor>
         <Key value="joe_authenticity_selfcare"> **Joe:** "…choosing not to drink in an alcohol-soaked culture is seen as an act of authenticity or self-care…"                                                                           </Key>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<Markdown when="@tmf_selfoptim_pick.value === 'danielle_health_research' &amp;&amp; @tmf_selfoptim_pick_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Not quite — Danielle's sentence says why health is on people's minds. Self-**optimization** needs a sentence about making yourself better, not about the research behind it.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmf_selfoptim_pick.value === 'karis_freedom_to_choose' &amp;&amp; @tmf_selfoptim_pick_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Not quite — "Freedom to choose" is about having the choice, not about what the choice does for you. Look for the sentence that says what not drinking makes a person.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmf_selfoptim_pick.value === 'joe_authenticity_selfcare' &amp;&amp; @tmf_selfoptim_pick_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Yes — "an act of authenticity or self-care" turns not drinking into working on yourself. That's the self-optimization the writer means.*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- tmf_selfoptim_pick_problem -->

<Markdown>**Explain why you chose that sentence.**</Markdown>
<TextArea id="tmf_selfoptim_explain" rows="3" placeholder="I chose that sentence because…" />
</Vertical> <!-- tmf_selfoptim -->

<!-- CHAT-EMBEDDED — summarize the first paragraph (PDF p.41) -->

<Vertical id="tmf_summary_pick_screen">
<CapaProblem id="tmf_summary_pick_problem" title="The best summary of the first paragraph" maxAttempts="1">
<KeyGrader id="tmf_summary_pick_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="tmf_summary_pick">
<Markdown>
Which is the **best summary** of the article's first paragraph (about Carrie
Nation)?
</Markdown>
         <Key value="what_and_why"       > In the early 1900s, Carrie Nation used bricks, stones, and a hatchet to destroy liquor bottles because she believed liquor caused "society's moral failings."                  </Key>
  <Distractor value="hatchet_detail_only"> Carrie Nation gained fame in 1900 for using a hatchet to break up liquor bottles in several saloons in the midwest.                                                            </Distractor>
  <Distractor value="vague_smashers"     > Starting in 1900, Carrie Nation invaded saloons and destroyed everything with "smashers."                                                                                      </Distractor>
  <Distractor value="opinion_interesting"> Carrie Nation is one of the most interesting figures of the early 1900s, mostly because she focused on getting physically (and violently) involved in the temperance movement. </Distractor>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<Markdown when="@tmf_summary_pick.value === 'hatchet_detail_only' &amp;&amp; @tmf_summary_pick_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Not quite — true, but only half the paragraph. A summary has to carry **why** she did it, not just what she swung.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmf_summary_pick.value === 'vague_smashers' &amp;&amp; @tmf_summary_pick_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Not quite — "Destroyed everything" overstates it, and the "smashers" were the women who joined her. A summary shouldn't blur the facts it reports.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmf_summary_pick.value === 'opinion_interesting' &amp;&amp; @tmf_summary_pick_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Not quite — that's an opinion about Carrie Nation, not a summary of the paragraph. Summaries report; they don't rate.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmf_summary_pick.value === 'what_and_why' &amp;&amp; @tmf_summary_pick_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Yes — what she did, how she did it, and the reason the paragraph gives: she blamed liquor for "society's moral failings."*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- tmf_summary_pick_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- tmf_summary_pick_screen -->

<!-- CHAT-EMBEDDED — article: "authenticity" vocab + overall thesis -->

<Vertical id="tmf_q_authenticity_screen">
<CapaProblem id="tmf_q_authenticity_problem" title="Vocabulary: authenticity" maxAttempts="1">
<KeyGrader id="tmf_q_authenticity_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="tmf_q_authenticity">
<Markdown>
The article says choosing not to drink is seen as "an act of **authenticity**
or self-care." What does **authenticity** mean here?
</Markdown>
         <Key value="true_to_self"       > Being true to who you really are.             </Key>
  <Distractor value="following_rules"    > Following a strict set of official rules.     </Distractor>
  <Distractor value="group_approval"     > Getting approval from a group before you act. </Distractor>
  <Distractor value="doing_whats_popular"> Doing whatever happens to be popular.         </Distractor>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<Markdown when="@tmf_q_authenticity.value === 'following_rules' &amp;&amp; @tmf_q_authenticity_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Not quite — rules arrive from outside you. The article pairs authenticity with self-care — something that comes from inside.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmf_q_authenticity.value === 'group_approval' &amp;&amp; @tmf_q_authenticity_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Not quite — nearly the opposite: the article's example is not drinking in "an alcohol-soaked culture" — without the group's blessing.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmf_q_authenticity.value === 'doing_whats_popular' &amp;&amp; @tmf_q_authenticity_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Not quite — popularity is everyone else's taste. Authenticity is your own.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmf_q_authenticity.value === 'true_to_self' &amp;&amp; @tmf_q_authenticity_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Right — being genuinely yourself. That's how turning down a drink becomes a statement about who you are.*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- tmf_q_authenticity_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- tmf_q_authenticity_screen -->

<Vertical id="tmf_article_thesis_screen">
<CapaProblem id="tmf_article_thesis_problem" title="The article's main point" maxAttempts="1">
<KeyGrader id="tmf_article_thesis_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="tmf_article_thesis">
<Markdown>
Step back from the details — what is the article's **main point** overall?
</Markdown>
  <Distractor value="nation_most_important"      > Carrie Nation was the single most important figure in the temperance movement, surpassing even Lyman Beecher.                                          </Distractor>
         <Key value="new_temperance_new_morality"> Today's "sober-curious" movement is a kind of new temperance, but its morality has shifted from protecting society to improving a person's own health. </Key>
  <Distractor value="more_dangerous_today"       > Drinking alcohol is far more dangerous today than it was in the 1800s because today's alcohol causes heart disease and cancer.                         </Distractor>
  <Distractor value="surgeon_general_prohibition"> The Surgeon General's goal in suggesting a warning label on alcohol is to bring back Prohibition.                                                      </Distractor>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<Markdown when="@tmf_article_thesis.value === 'nation_most_important' &amp;&amp; @tmf_article_thesis_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Not quite — she's the article's opening image, not its point. Ask what the writer is **using** her for.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmf_article_thesis.value === 'more_dangerous_today' &amp;&amp; @tmf_article_thesis_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Not quite — the article does report research on alcohol's harms, but it never claims today's alcohol is worse. That's a supporting detail.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmf_article_thesis.value === 'surgeon_general_prohibition' &amp;&amp; @tmf_article_thesis_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Not quite — another detail, and an overstatement — a warning label is not a ban.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmf_article_thesis.value === 'new_temperance_new_morality' &amp;&amp; @tmf_article_thesis_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Yes — same behavior, different reason. The 1800s movement set out to protect society; today's sets out to improve the self.*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- tmf_article_thesis_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- tmf_article_thesis_screen -->

<!-- CHAT-EMBEDDED — lithograph comprehension (Part 4) -->

<Vertical id="tmf_litho_observe_screen">
<CapaProblem id="tmf_litho_observe_problem" title="The lithograph: what it shows" maxAttempts="1">
<KeyGrader id="tmf_litho_observe_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="tmf_litho_observe">
<Markdown>
What does the image show across its nine numbered steps?
</Markdown>
  <Distractor value="top_is_moderate"   > That the best stage of becoming a drinker, at the top, is moderate drinking.                              </Distractor>
  <Distractor value="nine_different_men"> Nine different men, each working in a different profession that was common in the 1800s.                  </Distractor>
  <Distractor value="good_and_bad_sides"> The good and bad sides of drinking alcohol for working class people.                                      </Distractor>
         <Key value="one_mans_decline"  > One man's life of drinking, from a first drink, to the summit, to falling into poverty, crime, and death. </Key>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<Markdown when="@tmf_litho_observe.value === 'top_is_moderate' &amp;&amp; @tmf_litho_observe_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Not quite — read step 5's caption again: "the summit attained" is "a confirmed drunkard" — and it's where the fall starts.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmf_litho_observe.value === 'nine_different_men' &amp;&amp; @tmf_litho_observe_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Not quite — it's one man, nine times — the same life at nine moments. That's why it's called a "progress."*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmf_litho_observe.value === 'good_and_bad_sides' &amp;&amp; @tmf_litho_observe_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Not quite — the image is one-sided on purpose. It only rises in order to fall; nothing on the arch is offered as a good ending.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmf_litho_observe.value === 'one_mans_decline' &amp;&amp; @tmf_litho_observe_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Yes — one life, climbing to the summit and then dropping step by step to the grave.*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- tmf_litho_observe_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- tmf_litho_observe_screen -->

<Vertical id="tmf_litho_purpose_screen">
<CapaProblem id="tmf_litho_purpose_problem" title="The lithograph: why it was made" maxAttempts="1">
<KeyGrader id="tmf_litho_purpose_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="tmf_litho_purpose">
<Markdown>
Why would the temperance movement create and share an image like this?
</Markdown>
  <Distractor value="advertise_saloons" > To advertise the saloons pictured in it and encourage people to get together with their friends.          </Distractor>
  <Distractor value="record_social_life"> To record the kinds of social activities that a typical wealthy family participated in in 1846.           </Distractor>
         <Key value="warn_and_persuade" > To warn people that even a first drink can lead step by step to ruin — to persuade them to stop drinking. </Key>
  <Distractor value="no_consequences"   > To show that drinking has no lasting consequences as long as you drink moderately.                        </Distractor>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<Markdown when="@tmf_litho_purpose.value === 'advertise_saloons' &amp;&amp; @tmf_litho_purpose_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Not quite — an advertisement wouldn't run all the way to step 9. Everything past the summit is a warning.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmf_litho_purpose.value === 'record_social_life' &amp;&amp; @tmf_litho_purpose_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Not quite — it isn't a record of anything that happened. The ruined home beneath the arch is there to make you feel something.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmf_litho_purpose.value === 'no_consequences' &amp;&amp; @tmf_litho_purpose_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Not quite — the whole arch is consequences: poverty, disease, crime, death.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmf_litho_purpose.value === 'warn_and_persuade' &amp;&amp; @tmf_litho_purpose_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Yes — this is persuasion. Take one friendly glass, the picture argues, and the other eight steps follow.*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- tmf_litho_purpose_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- tmf_litho_purpose_screen -->

<!-- CHAT-EMBEDDED — synthesis: evaluate peer comparisons (Part 4) -->

<Vertical id="tmf_synth_peereval_screen">
<CapaProblem id="tmf_synth_peereval_problem" title="Then and now: the best comparison" maxAttempts="1">
<KeyGrader id="tmf_synth_peereval_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="tmf_synth_peereval">
<Markdown>
Your group each summed up the biggest difference between the two movements.
Which comparison is **best**?
</Markdown>
  <Distractor value="joe_not_different"       > **Joe:** They're actually NOT that different — both are about not drinking at all or drinking only moderately to protect society and families.                       </Distractor>
         <Key value="danielle_moral_vs_health"> **Danielle:** Both discourage drinking, but the 1800s movement was a moral and social crusade, while today's movement is about personal health and self-improvement. </Key>
  <Distractor value="karis_price_of_alcohol"  > **Karis:** The main difference is that people in the 1800s drank more expensive alcohol than people do today, which caused harm to a lot of families.                </Distractor>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<Markdown when="@tmf_synth_peereval.value === 'joe_not_different' &amp;&amp; @tmf_synth_peereval_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Not quite — Joe has the likeness right — but a comparison owes us the difference too, and these two movements give very different reasons for the same behavior.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmf_synth_peereval.value === 'karis_price_of_alcohol' &amp;&amp; @tmf_synth_peereval_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Not quite — price isn't a contrast either text draws — the textbook says whiskey was cheap. Compare what each movement was trying to protect.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmf_synth_peereval.value === 'danielle_moral_vs_health' &amp;&amp; @tmf_synth_peereval_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Yes — both discourage drinking; what changed is the reason. Society and family then, the self now. That's the comparison your final paragraph needs.*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- tmf_synth_peereval_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- tmf_synth_peereval_screen -->

</Vertical> <!-- temperance_module_full_activities_part1 -->
